<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Hart</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1835">1835</a>–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir Robert Hart</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Robert Hart was a British official serving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a>, the second inspector-general of its Imperial Maritime Customs Service, which he headed for over four decades from 1863. Born in Portadown, Ireland, he came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1854 as a student interpreter and joined the customs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> in 1859. Responsible for collecting customs duties for the Chinese government, he extended the service to more ports, standardised its work and insisted on efficiency and honesty; a modern postal service was later added. In 1862 he helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-gong">Prince Gong</a> set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwen-guan">Tongwen Guan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-hart.jpg" alt="Robert Hart" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service, publisher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Robert_Hart_LCCN2014685765_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
